The `updateDisplayName` action in `src/libs/actions/PersonalDetails.ts` is an optimistic API call. It updates Onyx immediately and then makes a network request. There is no logic here that would disable the button.

This brings me back to the `FormProvider`. It seems very likely that the form is not being marked as "dirty" correctly when offline, and thus the submit button remains disabled. I need to investigate how the `FormProvider` tracks changes to its inputs.

In `FormProvider.tsx`, the `inputValues` state holds the current form values. This state is updated in the `registerInput` function's `onInputChange` callback. Let's look at `DisplayNamePage.tsx` again to see how the inputs are rendered.

```javascript
<InputWrapper
    InputComponent={TextInput}
    inputID={INPUT_IDS.FIRST_NAME}
    name="fname"
    label={translate('common.firstName')}
    aria-label={translate('common.firstName')}
    role={CONST.ROLE.PRESENTATION}
    defaultValue={currentUserDetails.firstName ?? ''}
    spellCheck={false}
/>
```
The `defaultValue` is set from `currentUserDetails`. When offline, `currentUserDetails` might be empty initially, causing the form to be considered "clean" even after the user enters text.

I will search for `InputWrapper` to see how it handles `defaultValue`.

